<content>
  <section><title>Reading Exponential Graphs</title>
<para>The graphs of \(f\) and \(g\) are located below and are functions of the form \(y=a \cdot b^x\).</para>
<media><image alt="GRAPH OF EXPONENTIAL GROWTH FUNCTIONS, F AND G. FUNCTION G HAS A SMALLER \(y\)-intercepts AND CLIMBS MORE QUICKLY THAN FUNCTION F." src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cf82658767676ae9948f2725a0a00f2b82d786dc"/></media><para>Which curve has the larger \(a\) value? </para>
<para>\(f(x)\) because its \(y\)-intercept is higher.</para>
<para>Which curve has the larger \(b\) value?</para>
<para>\(g(x)\) because it is growing more quickly.</para>
<title>Try It: Reading Exponential Graphs</title>
<para>The graphs of \(f\) and \(g\) are located below and are functions of the form \(y=ab^x\).</para>
<media><image alt="GRAPH OF EXPONENTIAL GROWTH FUNCTIONS, F AND G. FUNCTION F HAS A SMALLER \(y\)-intercepts AND CLIMBS MORE QUICKLY THAN FUNCTION G." src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/0bb68cfe7139d9a4552aa673ce3ac2fb56238e98"/></media><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Which curve has the larger \(b\) value?</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(f(x)\) has the larger \(b\) value because it is growing faster than \(g(x)\).</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
